    "title": "‘Ozempic personality’: Some users reporting emotional blunting on GLP-1 drugs",
    "text": "by: Lily Dallow Posted: Apr 16, 2026 / 01:57 PM PDT Updated: Apr 16, 2026 / 01:57 PM PDT Some users of popular weight-loss drugs such as Ozempic are reporting a possible side effect being referred to online as “Ozempic personality,” a term describing a reduced ability to feel pleasure or emotional connection, according to a report from The Washington Post. Doctors say the symptoms align with anhedonia, a clinical term describing diminished enjoyment in activities that would normally bring happiness — from socializing and exercising to music, food or romantic relationships. GLP-1 medications, including semaglutide-based drugs such as Ozempic and Wegovy, are designed to regulate blood sugar and appetite by mimicking hormones in the body. While widely considered safe and effective for treating diabetes and obesity, researchers say the drugs’ potential psychological effects are still being studied. Some physicians report hearing anecdotal accounts of emotional “flattening,” in which patients say they still recognize positive moments but feel less excitement or connection to them. The phenomenon has gained attention on social media, where some users have labeled the experience “Ozempic personality.” Researchers believe the effect could be linked to how GLP-1 medications influence dopamine, a neurotransmitter involved in the brain’s reward system. By reducing food cravings and potentially other substances such as alcohol or nicotine, the drugs may also dampen other reward responses in some individuals, experts say. Still, specialists emphasize the medications remain generally safe and may even provide mental health benefits for some patients. Studies have suggested GLP-1 drugs may be associated with lower risks of worsening depression, anxiety and substance-use disorders, though researchers caution more data is needed to fully understand the relationship. Doctors say reports of emotional blunting are not considered widespread, but patients experiencing noticeable mood changes should consult their healthcare provider. In some cases, adjusting dosage may help symptoms improve. Experts stress patients should not stop taking prescribed medication without first speaking with a medical professional.",
